

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Good. Due date is the subscription, one global lead time.

  Last question before I lay out the design — the stack, because it's the one remaining choice that constrains
  everything downstream and this repo currently has no tooling at all.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether this stays a file you can open directly, or gains a build step.

  - [1mZero-build vanilla ES modules[0m — plain JS modules loaded from [38;5;153mindex.html[39m, no install, no [38;5;153mnode_modules[39m. Buys: you can
    double-click the file and it works; deployment is copying a directory. Costs: no types, and testing the time logic
    means hand-rolling a runner or skipping tests. Reversible, but converting later means touching every file.
  - [1mVite + TypeScript, no UI framework[0m — a real dev server, types, and Vitest with fake timers. Buys: the due-date and
    scheduling logic is exactly the code that rewards types and unit tests — off-by-one lead times, overdue transitions,
    and "what happens when the tab was closed for six hours" are all cheap to test with fake clocks and genuinely
    annoying to verify by hand. Costs: a node toolchain and config in a repo that currently holds one file.
  - [1mVite + a UI framework (React/Svelte)[0m — the above plus declarative rendering. Buys: less manual DOM code as the list
    grows. Costs: a dependency and a paradigm for a UI that is, realistically, a list and a panel.

  [1mMy recommendation: Vite + TypeScript, no framework.[0m The interesting part of this project is time logic, not rendering,
  and that's the part I want under test with a controllable clock. The DOM surface here is small enough that
  hand-written rendering stays readable, so a framework would be cost without much return.
